 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "A concrete bridge weighs hundreds of tons. So how does a flood just push it off its pillars?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Normally the river flows far below the deck. No problem.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But in a big flood, the water rises, and rises, until it hits the deck itself.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now the deck is a wall standing in a fast river. Every cubic meter of water weighs a ton.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then the floating trash arrives. Branches, logs, even cars.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "They jam against the railings and the pillars, and the wall gets bigger and bigger.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And here's the sneaky part. Air gets trapped under the deck, between the beams.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That air works like a life jacket. It lifts the deck up.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Lighter on top, pushed hard from the side. The deck slides right off.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So engineers fight back. First, they bolt the deck down to the pillars with steel ties.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "They punch vent holes in the deck, so trapped air can escape.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "They use open railings, so branches slide through instead of piling up.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And when they can, they simply build the deck higher than the worst flood on record.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Next time you cross a river, look at the gap under the bridge. That gap is the plan.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
